{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779385813,"nonce":"im/YqgCaASCTjb43Mh2mtLRzpGc=","title":"Studio Workspace spatial representation and objective authoring contract","message":"## Problem\n\nThe current Workspace projection should not be treated as an already-designed surface that only needs wiring. It is still mostly a schematic/read-only projection. The missing work is a representation and authoring contract for what tasks, mechanics, effectors, objectives, and future components mean geometrically inside the Workspace tab.\n\nStudio needs consistent ways to answer: what is the actual geometric presence of this mechanics object, task object, or component in the Workspace, and what interaction target does the user manipulate when authoring objectives or bindings?\n\nThis issue is the concrete child of `a3e46d5` for Workspace spatial representation and authoring. Runtime playback and live validation overlays are related but should be tracked separately unless an implementation slice proves they can share the exact same data contract.\n\n## Design Scope\n\nThe Workspace projection needs a typed spatial representation layer over `StudioScenarioSpec`, not a collection of bespoke drawings. It should support both literal and schematic representations:\n\n- literal geometry, such as a two-link arm, joints, fixed/mobile anchors, links, effectors, workspace bounds, and obstacles;\n- schematic geometry, such as center-out task targets, random reach distributions, task phases, perturbation loci, or representative task points;\n- interaction targets, such as the canonical goal point used when drawing an objective, even if several schematic points are visible;\n- provider-declared renderer/representation metadata for shipped tasks, mechanics, and components;\n- consistent selectors back to task/mechanics/objective/spec entities so edits mutate the scenario, not canvas-only state.\n\n## Current Mockup Boundary\n\nFuture implementation should start from the assumption that the existing Workspace projection is not a finished UX. It may contain useful scaffolding, but it does not yet define:\n\n- which spatial objects are canonical versus illustrative;\n- which visible objects can participate in objective creation;\n- how task-specific schematic objects map back to task parameters;\n- how mechanics-specific geometry maps back to model/mechanics state;\n- what metadata a new provider task/mechanics/component must supply to render correctly.\n\n## Required Concepts\n\n### Spatial representation records\n\nStudio should have an intermediate representation, tentatively `WorkspaceRepresentationSpec` or similar, derived from provider/schema data plus active scenario state. It should describe visible spatial entities with:\n\n- stable id and source selector/spec path;\n- semantic kind: effector, joint, link, anchor, body, goal, target distribution, trajectory, obstacle, cue, perturbation, task region, annotation, objective edge, etc.;\n- display mode: literal, schematic, representative, sampled-preview, or runtime-overlay;\n- geometry primitive: point, polyline, segment chain, circle, region, vector, distribution glyph, image/mesh reference, etc.;\n- coordinate frame, units, scale, and workspace bounds;\n- interaction role: selectable, draggable, objective-source, objective-target, binding target, read-only, disabled, or illustrative;\n- canonicalization rule for objective/binding creation;\n- validation state and compatibility metadata.\n\n### Mechanics representation\n\nMechanics nodes need provider-declared spatial renderers or representation metadata. Examples:\n\n- point mass: a point, likely with the effector role distinguished from the body marker;\n- two-link arm: two links with accurate geometry, joint markers, fixed base, mobile joints, and a consistent effector marker;\n- muscle/actuator mechanics: optional line-of-action or attachment geometry when available;\n- future acausal/mechanics subgraphs: a scoped representation that can be literal when geometry is known or schematic when only role/topology is known.\n\nEffectors should have a consistent visual role across mechanics types, because objective authoring often starts from the effector.\n\n### Task representation\n\nTasks also need provider-declared schematic/literal representations. Examples:\n\n- center-out reaching: show the center and target directions schematically, but designate one canonical goal target for objective creation. If the user starts an objective from another visible center-out target marker, the resulting objective should canonicalize to the task's goal entity rather than creating a separate objective to a decorative point.\n- random reaches in a rectangular workspace: represent the distribution schematically, perhaps as one representative point or separate initial/target points depending on the task semantics.\n- tasks with separate initial and target states: use distinguishable shapes/roles for start and goal, with selectors and objective roles kept explicit.\n- delayed tasks: show task phases/cues only when they are spatially meaningful; temporal details remain coordinated with the task timeline/value editor under `bd5aefb`.\n\nTask-specific renderer metadata may be necessary. The contract should allow common representation families, but not pretend every task can be inferred from generic scalar fields.\n\n### Objective authoring\n\nWorkspace interactions should create or edit real objective/loss/metric specs. For example:\n\n- selecting an effector and drawing to the task goal should initiate a goal-state or endpoint-error objective;\n- if the selected schematic task marker is not canonical, the created objective should bind to the canonical task goal selector while the UI explains or visually snaps to that target;\n- objective overlays should be projections of objective specs, not separate drawing objects;\n- compatibility checks should use `ValueSchema`, `SelectorTargetSchema`, units/frames, and task/mechanics metadata.\n\n## Provider and Mandible Implications\n\nThis cannot be Studio-only if agents or Mandible will ship new tasks, mechanics, components, or provider extensions.\n\nThe provider contract should eventually expose enough representation metadata for Studio and external agents to understand how to render and interact with new domain objects. This may include:\n\n- workspace renderer ids or declarative representation specs in task/mechanics/component registry entries;\n- geometry schemas for common primitives and coordinate frames;\n- canonical interaction targets for objectives and bindings;\n- task-specific schematic rendering hooks where generic geometry is insufficient;\n- validation errors when a provider object is executable but not representable in Workspace;\n- fallbacks that are honest: unavailable, schematic-only, or debug/metadata-only.\n\nMandible should not hardcode Feedbax Workspace rendering. It should consume provider capabilities or open the object in Feedbax Studio when Feedbax supplies the representation contract.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Full 3D/CAD mechanics editing.\n- Fully general acausal/DAE system rendering.\n- Live trajectory playback during training or evaluation. That belongs in a related runtime-overlay issue, though both should share spatial entity ids and coordinate-frame metadata where possible.\n- Arbitrary custom JavaScript renderers from unreviewed provider packages.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A dedicated `WorkspaceRepresentationSpec` or equivalent data model exists for Workspace-visible entities.\n- Point mass and two-link arm mechanics have consistent spatial representations, including effectors and joints where applicable.\n- At least one center-out task and one random/reach-distribution-style task have schematic representations with explicit canonical objective targets.\n- Workspace objective creation writes to durable objective/task/mechanics specs and canonical selector refs.\n- Non-canonical schematic targets can still initiate objective creation, but the resulting spec points at the canonical target and the UI reflects that canonicalization.\n- Provider/schema registry records can declare or derive the Workspace representation metadata needed by built-in tasks/mechanics.\n- Missing representation metadata fails gracefully and visibly rather than pretending a raw field dump is a real Workspace visualization.\n\n## Related Issues\n\n- `a3e46d5` — umbrella sequencing ledger for Studio product-depth and Feedbax contracts.\n- `93f79f2` — Studio design coordination.\n- `8d94424` — core Feedbax design coordination.\n- `bd5aefb` — task timelines and value/function/distribution authoring.\n- `0258fac` — visible port/type/schema compatibility affordances.\n- `0e1cd8c` — Probe/Metric nodes and loss targets.\n- `b3ad156` — flexible intervention targeting.\n- `6116155` — typed subgraph taxonomy and pluggable canvas architecture.\n- `c6c6da0` — provider/manifests/schema contract.\n- `f68cf66` — Feedbax as a Mandible provider/plugin.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}